1
00:00:00,244 --> 00:00:03,536
In 2019, the best AI could handle three-second

2
00:00:03,546 --> 00:00:04,324
tasks.

3
00:00:04,525 --> 00:00:06,069
Today, twelve-hour ones.

4
00:00:06,476 --> 00:00:07,761
METR measures it:

5
00:00:08,045 --> 00:00:09,509
how long a human expert takes on

6
00:00:09,519 --> 00:00:11,525
the tasks the AI completes half the

7
00:00:11,535 --> 00:00:12,077
time.

8
00:00:12,504 --> 00:00:15,157
That length doubles roughly every seven months.

9
00:00:15,358 --> 00:00:17,704
And since 2023, every four.

10
00:00:18,136 --> 00:00:22,466
Seconds with GPT-2, minutes with GPT-4, an

11
00:00:22,476 --> 00:00:24,055
hour in 2025…

12
00:00:24,068 --> 00:00:25,317
and twelve hours today.

13
00:00:25,748 --> 00:00:26,431
But careful:

14
00:00:26,597 --> 00:00:28,106
that's succeeding half the time.

15
00:00:28,295 --> 00:00:30,325
Ask for 80%, and it drops to

16
00:00:30,335 --> 00:00:31,420
just over an hour.

17
00:00:31,848 --> 00:00:33,996
And these are software tasks, cleaner than

18
00:00:34,006 --> 00:00:35,895
real work, with a huge margin of

19
00:00:35,905 --> 00:00:36,400
error:

20
00:00:36,577 --> 00:00:38,275
from 5 to 60 hours.

21
00:00:38,704 --> 00:00:40,027
So don't ask whether AI can do

22
00:00:40,037 --> 00:00:40,708
your job.

23
00:00:40,898 --> 00:00:43,258
Ask which tasks, how many hours, and

24
00:00:43,268 --> 00:00:44,234
how reliably.

25
00:00:44,648 --> 00:00:46,523
The stopwatch is the right instrument.

26
00:00:46,724 --> 00:00:47,784
But it's not a verdict.

27
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:49,680
Save it, and share it with someone

28
00:00:49,690 --> 00:00:51,340
who thinks AI has stalled.

